;; Metistry reconciler — host confinement profile.
;;
;; The reconciler is the SOLE COMMITTER (D5): the only process that holds the
;; instance repo's working tree and the only place git runs. Until this file
;; existed it was also the only child of the supervisor with no boundary at
;; all — it could read every note in the vault (which is its job) and also
;; ~/Documents, ~/.ssh and the rest of the disk (which is not).
;; docs/research/2026-09-19-agent-virtual-filesystems.md §3.2(c) called it
;; "the best target in the repo"; this profile is that finding built.
;;
;; What changes for the reader: D5 stops being a design intention and becomes
;; something the kernel enforces. CLAUDE.md's governing principle — "enforce
;; at the tool, never by prompting … the tool must be incapable of it" —
;; applied to the one process that can rewrite the record.
;;
;; Invoked by ops/launchd/com.foldedspacelabs.metistry.reconciler.plist as
;;   /usr/bin/sandbox-exec -f <this file> -D KEY=VALUE … <node> <main.js>
;; so the sandbox is the job's root process and every child — git and all
;; 172 of its own helpers — inherits it (sandbox(7): "New processes inherit
;; the sandbox of their parent"; a nested sandbox-exec cannot loosen OR
;; tighten it).
;;
;; Parameters (all absolute, all computed in packages/cli/src/sandbox.ts,
;; which is the one place they exist):
;;   NODE_PREFIX     the node installation root (read + exec)
;;   NODE_BIN        the node binary itself (read + exec)
;;   PRODUCT_DIR     the product checkout: dist/, node_modules/, seed/ (read
;;                   only) — and the job's WorkingDirectory, which matters:
;;                   a confined process whose cwd it cannot read dies with
;;                   `fatal: Unable to read current working directory`
;;   INSTANCE_DIR    the instance repo — the vault AND .metistry/. The one
;;                   WRITABLE location besides TMP_DIR, and the whole point:
;;                   the sole committer may write the record and nothing else
;;   TMP_DIR         the per-user temporary directory
;;   GIT_PREFIX      a REAL git's installation prefix (read + exec) — see
;;                   "the git shim" below, which is the finding that cost the
;;                   most to learn
;;   GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL  the user's ~/.gitconfig, BY NAME (read only)
;;   ASKPASS_BIN     the askpass shim `metistry up` generates (read + exec,
;;                   by LITERAL) — see "the credential" below
;;   RECONCILER_TCP  the bridge port this process binds, e.g. localhost:7812
;;   CONSOLE_TCP     the console's loopback endpoint
;;   DB_TCP          Postgres' loopback endpoint (the reconciler holds its
;;                   own pool — apps/reconciler/src/main.ts)
;;   EMBED_TCP       the on-machine embedding server's loopback endpoint
;;                   (llama-server, LM Studio, Ollama, apple-fm). Absent an
;;                   embedder the rule grants a port nothing answers on,
;;                   which is the honest default: the index degrades, the
;;                   profile does not change shape with a config file
;;   PROXY_TCP       the supervisor's egress proxy (packages/core/src/
;;                   egress.ts). The ONLY route off this machine.
;;
;; THE GIT SHIM — measured on macOS 26.4 (25E246), 2026-09-19, and the
;; opposite of what the research doc guessed. `/usr/bin/git` is NOT a real
;; git: `otool -L` shows it linked against `/usr/lib/libxcselect.dylib`, i.e.
;; it is the xcode-select shim, and under a profile that allows exactly that
;; one literal it fails with
;;   xcrun: error: unable to load libxcrun (dlopen(/Applications/Xcode.app/
;;   Contents/Developer/usr/lib/libxcrun.dylib …): file system sandbox
;;   blocked open())
;; So the profile names a real git's PREFIX instead — `bin/git`,
;; `libexec/git-core/` (172 helpers, including git-remote-http(s)) and
;; `share/git-core/templates`. `metistry up` resolves it: the bundled
;; runtime's `runtime/git` first, then the Command Line Tools, then a git on
;; PATH that is not the shim. If it finds none it refuses to confine this job
;; and says so, rather than installing a reconciler that cannot commit.
;;
;; WHAT THIS PROFILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT ALLOW, and what it costs:
;;
;;   no shell. There is no /bin/sh and no /bin/bash here, which is invariant
;;   9's posture applied to the committer. Measured consequence: git runs
;;   EVERY credential helper through the shell — even the built-in
;;   `osxkeychain` that lives inside GIT_PREFIX — so a confined reconciler
;;   cannot use one (`fatal: cannot exec 'git credential-osxkeychain get':
;;   Operation not permitted`; granting /bin/sh is not enough either, because
;;   macOS's /bin/sh re-execs /bin/bash). That is what ASKPASS_BIN above is
;;   for, and HTTPS push works confined because of it: `up` runs git with
;;   `-c credential.helper=` (git's documented reset, so the repo's
;;   osxkeychain line cannot fail first) and GIT_ASKPASS pointing at the
;;   shim. Proven by a real push to a bare repo over HTTPS through the egress
;;   proxy, under this profile (packages/cli/test/reconciler-push.test.ts).
;;
;;   no ssh. `/usr/bin/ssh` is not exec-able here. Allowing it would mean
;;   granting the sole committer read of ~/.ssh — the owner's private keys —
;;   and ssh's ProxyCommand runs through the shell besides, so it could not
;;   reach the egress proxy anyway. SSH remotes are unsupported while
;;   confined; `metistry up` says so when it sees one, and doctor carries the
;;   row. docs/ops/reconciler.md has the whole table.
;;
;; sandbox-exec(1) is deprecated by Apple and still functional (verified on
;; macOS 26.4; the same profile shape has worked since 14). The migration
;; path is App Sandbox entitlements once the Mac app hosts this process —
;; with the honest caveat ops/sandbox/README.md now states: App Sandbox buys
;; host-name egress filtering and COSTS the per-spawn `-D` parameterisation
;; every rule below depends on. It is a trade, not a pure upgrade.

(version 1)
(deny default)

;; Denials are reported to the system log either way — a violation we did
;; not predict is findable with
;;   log stream --predicate 'sender == "Sandbox"'

(import "system.sb")

(allow process-fork)

;; ---- exec ---------------------------------------------------------------
;; Three programs: node, a real git, and the askpass shim. No shell.
;;
;; THE CREDENTIAL. git runs every credential helper through /bin/sh — even
;; the built-in osxkeychain `metistry connect-repo` configures — so no helper
;; can run here (measured; see below). `GIT_ASKPASS` can: git execs it
;; DIRECTLY, by absolute path, with no shell, and this exec allowlist is the
;; only gate. So `up` generates a `#!<node>` shim, grants it by literal, and
;; the supervisor — unconfined, and the parent — reads the token out of the
;; login Keychain once and hands it to this process in its environment. The
;; shim prints one of two environment variables and can do nothing else:
;; it takes no path and opens no file (packages/cli/src/askpass.ts).
;;
;; ASKPASS_BIN lives under INSTANCE_DIR, which this process can write. That
;; is not a hole: NODE_BIN is exec-allowed and node runs whatever JavaScript
;; it can read, so "arbitrary code inside the sandbox" was never what this
;; profile prevented — reaching OUTSIDE is, and a fourteen-line shim changes
;; nothing about that.
(allow process-exec*
  (literal (param "NODE_BIN"))
  (literal (param "ASKPASS_BIN"))
  (subpath (param "NODE_PREFIX"))
  (subpath (param "GIT_PREFIX")))

;; ---- reads --------------------------------------------------------------
;; Metadata (lstat) on any path: node resolves its entry point by lstat-ing
;; every ancestor directory, and git stats its way up looking for a repo
;; root. Metadata is existence and size, never content.
(allow file-read-metadata)

;; System frameworks, the certificate store, timezone and resolver config.
(allow file-read*
  (subpath "/usr/lib")
  (subpath "/usr/share")
  (subpath "/System")
  (subpath "/Library/Preferences/Logging")
  (subpath "/private/var/db/timezone")
  (subpath "/private/etc/ssl")
  (literal "/private/etc/hosts")
  (literal "/private/etc/resolv.conf")
  (literal "/private/etc/localtime")
  (literal "/private/etc/protocols")
  (literal "/private/etc/services"))

;; Its own code and runtime, and a real git — all read-only.
(allow file-read*
  (literal (param "NODE_BIN"))
  (literal (param "ASKPASS_BIN"))
  (subpath (param "NODE_PREFIX"))
  (subpath (param "PRODUCT_DIR"))
  (subpath (param "GIT_PREFIX")))

;; The user's ~/.gitconfig, BY NAME. Not the home directory — one file.
;;
;; This rule is not optional politeness: git treats an UNREADABLE global
;; config as fatal rather than absent —
;;   fatal: unable to access '/Users/…/.gitconfig': Operation not permitted
;; — so without it the reconciler cannot run `git status`, let alone commit.
;; (Measured 2026-09-19.) The file is where the owner's identity and their
;; `credential.*` blocks live; it is read, never written, and a helper named
;; in it still cannot run (no shell).
(allow file-read* (literal (param "GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL")))

;; ---- writes -------------------------------------------------------------
;; Exactly two: the instance repo (the vault, `.metistry/`, and `.git/` —
;; this IS the sole committer, and a committer that cannot write the record
;; is not one) and the temporary directory.
;;
;; Everything else on this Mac is now out of reach, which is the whole
;; change: ~/Documents, ~/.ssh, the product checkout, another instance's
;; vault, and the launch agents directory.
(allow file-read* file-write*
  (subpath (param "INSTANCE_DIR"))
  (subpath (param "TMP_DIR")))

;; ---- network ------------------------------------------------------------
;; It listens on ONE port — its own bridge — and dials four destinations,
;; three of them loopback.
;;
;; Listening takes TWO rules on macOS 26, not one. `network-bind` lets the
;; socket take the address; `listen()` is then refused with EPERM unless
;; `network-inbound` names the same local address. Measured on Darwin 25.4,
;; 2026-09-28: with the bind rule alone, `createServer().listen(7812,
;; "127.0.0.1")` fails `listen EPERM`, the reconciler crash-loops, and
;; nobody saw it before 0.14.2 because earlier installs had no real git and
;; so ran unconfined.sb. ops/sandbox/bind.test.mjs listens under this exact
;; profile on every CI run that touches it, so this cannot quietly regress.
(allow network-bind (local ip (param "RECONCILER_TCP")))
(allow network-inbound (local ip (param "RECONCILER_TCP")))

;; 1. The console, on loopback.
(allow network-outbound (remote tcp (param "CONSOLE_TCP")))

;; 2. Postgres, on loopback: the reconciler holds its own pool for the
;;    derived index and the embeddings table.
(allow network-outbound (remote tcp (param "DB_TCP")))

;; 3. The on-machine embedding server, on loopback.
(allow network-outbound (remote tcp (param "EMBED_TCP")))

;; 4. The supervisor's egress proxy — the ONE way off this machine, and the
;;    only reason `*:443` is not here. The proxy takes a CONNECT, checks the
;;    host against a list `metistry up` derived from this install's git
;;    remotes and compute.yaml, authenticates the child, and refuses
;;    everything else with a `runs` row (packages/core/src/egress.ts).
;;    `git push` reaches it through `http.proxy`, which `up` sets on this
;;    job. Measured: a non-allowlisted remote comes back as
;;    `fatal: … CONNECT tunnel failed, response 403`, and with no proxy
;;    configured at all this rule alone produces
;;    `Failed to connect to github.com port 443 … Couldn't connect to server`.
(allow network-outbound (remote tcp (param "PROXY_TCP")))

;; DNS resolution goes through mDNSResponder, not a socket to :53. Kept
;; because node's startup and git's own resolver touch it; it resolves names
;; but reaches nothing — the four rules above are still the whole of where
;; this process can connect.
(allow network-outbound (remote unix-socket (path-literal "/private/var/run/mDNSResponder")))
(allow mach-lookup
  (global-name "com.apple.dnssd.service")
  (global-name "com.apple.system.opendirectoryd.libinfo")
  (global-name "com.apple.SystemConfiguration.configd")
  (global-name "com.apple.SystemConfiguration.DNSConfiguration")
  (global-name "com.apple.SystemConfiguration.PPPController")
  (global-name "com.apple.SystemConfiguration.NetworkInformation")
  (global-name "com.apple.nehelper")
  (global-name "com.apple.networkd")
  (global-name "com.apple.usymptomsd"))

;; Reading the machine's own network/system state (node's os module, git's
;; core.fsmonitor probe).
(allow sysctl-read)
(allow system-socket)
